  ],
  "text": "Put fourteen wolves back into a valley and the rivers physically change course. It starts with something very ordinary. Wolves eat elk. So elk stop grazing the riverbanks, because standing still there gets you killed. Willows and aspens start growing back along the water again. Beavers return, because suddenly there is something worth building with. The dams slow the water down, and the banks stop washing away. And the river changes shape. Fewer straight runs, more bends. But researchers still argue about how much of this the wolves actually did. Drought was in there too. The honest version is still the remarkable one. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
